Understanding the OSI model helps map where attacks happen and how to defend effectively.

#1️⃣ Physical Layer
⚠️ Attacks: Cable tapping, device theft, hardware tampering
🛡️ Controls: Physical security, CCTV, access control

#2️⃣ Data Link Layer
⚠️ Attacks: ARP spoofing, MAC flooding, VLAN hopping
🛡️ Controls: Port security, ARP inspection, VLAN hardening

#3️⃣ Network Layer
⚠️ Attacks: IP spoofing, routing attacks, ICMP floods
🛡️ Controls: Firewalls, ACLs, IPS, network segmentation

#4️⃣ Transport Layer
⚠️ Attacks: SYN floods, session hijacking, port scanning
🛡️ Controls: Stateful firewalls, rate limiting, IDS/IPS

#5️⃣ Session Layer
⚠️ Attacks: Session hijacking, replay attacks
🛡️ Controls: Secure session management, timeouts, encryption

#6️⃣ Presentation Layer
⚠️ Attacks: SSL/TLS downgrade, encryption abuse
🛡️ Controls: Strong encryption, certificate management

#7️⃣ Application Layer
⚠️ Attacks: SQL injection, XSS, CSRF, malware
🛡️ Controls: WAF, secure coding, patching, input validation
